---
/*
  Tell me your dev disaster.

  The form posts to /api/disasters/, which lands in phase five along with sign in. It is
  a real form with real fields rather than a script driven one, so the no JavaScript path
  is the only path and there is nothing to degrade.

  The mockup's rail said "TITLE: I'll write it" and the notice said "I write a title".
  Decision 25 says the title and slug are generated on submit and editable afterwards, so
  the copy here says that instead. A public promise the system will not keep is worse
  than a slightly less charming sentence.

  The anonymity control is a checkbox, not a button with aria-checked. A button needs
  script to change state, and this page is not allowed to need script.
*/
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Tell me your Dev disaster"
  active="dd"
  description="Somewhere in your git history is a commit you still think about. Write it down."
>
  <main id="main">
    <div class="shell read">
      <aside class="rail">
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Sign in</dt><dd>GitHub</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Review time</dt><dd>Usually a day</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Title</dt><dd>Drafted for you</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Length</dt><dd>Whatever it takes</dd></div>
        </dl>
        <span class="fine">Nothing publishes until I've read it.</span>
      </aside>

      <article>
        <header class="storyhead">
          <span class="path">dev-disasters<span class="sep">/</span><b>new</b></span>
          <h1>What's the one you <span class="squiggle"><span>still think about?</span></span></h1>
          <p class="lede">
            No form fields to fight with. No severity to pick. Just tell it the way you'd
            tell it to somebody at a bar who also writes code.
          </p>
        </header>

        <form class="form submit-form" method="post" action="/api/disasters/">
          <div class="field">
            <label for="story">The story</label>
            <p class="help">
              Start wherever it starts. What broke, what you tried, what actually fixed it,
              and what you do differently now. Markdown works, and code fences are welcome
              if the query is the punchline.
            </p>
            <textarea
              id="story"
              name="story"
              rows="14"
              required
              placeholder="It was a Tuesday and I was the guy who knew the database..."></textarea>
            <p class="help count">No minimum, no maximum</p>
          </div>

          <div class="switch-row">
            <input class="switch" type="checkbox" id="anon" name="anonymous" checked />
            <div>
              <label class="switch-label" for="anon">Publish anonymously</label>
              <span class="switch-help">
                Your name and handle stay off the page. You still get the badge, and I can
                still email you if the story becomes a video.
              </span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="field">
            <label>Before you send it <span class="opt">plain English, no lawyers</span></label>
            <label class="consent">
              <input type="checkbox" name="consent_scrubbed" required />
              <span>
                This happened to me, or I was there for it. I've taken out anything that
                would identify my employer, my coworkers, or a customer. If I missed
                something, take it out for me.
              </span>
            </label>
            <label class="consent">
              <input type="checkbox" name="consent_publish" required />
              <span>
                You can publish it here, edit it for length and clarity but never for
                meaning, and talk about it in a video. I keep the right to ask you to take
                it down, and you'll do it.
              </span>
            </label>
          </div>

          <p class="notice">
            Here's what happens next. It lands in a queue only I can see, with a title and
            a web address drafted for it automatically. I read it, fix the title if the
            machine got it wrong, check it for anything that identifies somebody, and file
            it under a severity. You get an email when it goes live, and another one if it
            turns into a video. If I decide not to run it, you get that email too, with the
            reason.
          </p>

          <div class="form-foot">
            <button class="btn primary" type="submit">Send it in</button>
            <span class="fine">You can edit it until I publish it.</span>
          </div>

          <p class="fine submit-fine">
            The long version lives in the <a href="/terms/">submission terms</a>. It says
            the same thing with more commas. The <a href="/conduct/">code of conduct</a>
            applies here too.
          </p>
        </form>
      </article>
    </div>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  .submit-form {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
  }
  .submit-fine {
    margin-top: -0.5rem;
  }
</style>
